1
00:00:00,760 --> 00:00:08,460
The task, monumental, build the world's largest dam and do it in less than seven years.

2
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:10,600
Nothing like it had ever been attempted.

3
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:14,860
Now, Hoover Dam on Modern Marbles.

4
00:00:14,861 --> 00:00:28,861
Located in the middle of America's Southwest Desert

5
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:34,020
stands a monumental product of human ingenuity and accomplishment.

6
00:00:34,021 --> 00:00:39,640
A vessel of life-giving water and a structure of awe-inspiring size.

7
00:00:39,641 --> 00:00:42,660
726 feet high

8
00:00:42,661 --> 00:00:45,960
weighing 6.6 million tons

9
00:00:45,961 --> 00:00:48,540
and creating a lake

10
00:00:48,541 --> 00:00:50,380
150 miles long

11
00:00:50,381 --> 00:00:52,600
Hoover Dam is impressive

12
00:00:52,601 --> 00:00:54,060
and controversial

13
00:00:54,061 --> 00:00:56,580
so were the people who built it

14
00:00:56,581 --> 00:00:58,860
personalities such as

15
00:00:58,861 --> 00:01:00,320
the stern Sims Ely

16
00:01:00,321 --> 00:01:01,720
the law of the land

17
00:01:01,721 --> 00:01:03,180
and Frank Crow

18
00:01:03,181 --> 00:01:05,840
the feared and respected head of construction

19
00:01:05,841 --> 00:01:08,640
thousands labored

20
00:01:08,641 --> 00:01:14,520
to build Hoover Dam, workers constantly struggled against the environment and often their employers.

21
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:22,200
The damming of the Colorado River created Lake Mead, the largest man-made reservoir in the world.

22
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:29,420
Ironically, it's located in one of the driest places on earth, 30 miles southeast of Las Vegas,

23
00:01:29,421 --> 00:01:30,121
Nevada.

24
00:01:30,122 --> 00:01:36,800
Hoover Dam symbolizes the human spirit's discontent to accept nature's rules.

25
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:41,700
Building the Hoover Dam was a colossal undertaking in 1931,

26
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:44,340
the largest government project ever attempted.

27
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:47,400
Constructed in the depths of the Depression,

28
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:49,940
its purpose was to provide flood control,

29
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:53,500
irrigation, drinking water,

30
00:01:54,060 --> 00:01:55,540
and electricity.

31
00:01:57,680 --> 00:02:01,640
It promised to change barren to bountiful.

32
00:02:04,420 --> 00:02:06,600
Water is essential to life.

33
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:11,200
And nowhere is that better understood than in the dry climate of the American Southwest.

34
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:14,080
The Southwest is an arid area.

35
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:21,140
And without a dam like Hoover Dam to control the flow of the river and make the water available when it's needed,

36
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:25,780
we just couldn't have the level of economic development that we have in the Southwest today.

37
00:02:25,781 --> 00:02:31,500
Thanks to the Hoover Dam, the Colorado River began irrigating more than a million acres of farmland.

38
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:33,120
Cities sprouted.

39
00:02:33,121 --> 00:02:36,940
And Las Vegas became Las Vegas.

40
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:42,900
Hoover Dam is one of the primary attractions for those visiting Las Vegas.

41
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:47,280
At over 60 years old, it hasn't lost its appeal.

42
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:50,980
More than one million people visit the dam a year.

43
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:58,220
Overwhelming in its scope and size, it draws visitors from around the world.

44
00:02:58,340 --> 00:03:02,260
The dam's enormity is matched only by its dramatic history.

45
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:07,260
The Hoover Dam was the first major attempt to tame the mighty Colorado River,

46
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:09,860
the same river that carved the Grand Canyon.

47
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:14,980
The 1,400-mile river drains water from seven states,

48
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:21,740
Wyoming, Nevada, Utah, Colorado, California, Arizona, and New Mexico,

49
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:25,640
and delivers its precious payload to the Gulf of California.

50
00:03:27,260 --> 00:03:32,300
Snow melting in the Rocky Mountains sends a torrent of water down the Colorado every spring,

51
00:03:32,301 --> 00:03:34,840
but then dries to a trickle by summer.

52
00:03:35,860 --> 00:03:39,260
The early settlers who were trying to build an economy,

53
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:41,560
built lives at the bottom end of this river,

54
00:03:41,561 --> 00:03:45,540
they planted their crops and built their fields and built homes and businesses.

55
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:49,660
In the spring times, those would be subject to inundation

56
00:03:49,661 --> 00:03:52,860
or destruction because of the floods coming down the river.

57
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:57,140
And by the end of the summer, when the farmers really needed that water

58
00:03:57,141 --> 00:04:00,480
to get their crops to maturity, they couldn't get to the water.

59
00:04:00,481 --> 00:04:11,580
They needed a way to regulate the flow of the river, holding back the devastating floodwater in spring and delivering the life-giving water in the dry summer.

60
00:04:12,540 --> 00:04:16,260
Men's attempts to harness the power of the Colorado River date back a century.

61
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:26,410
In 1901, a private company constructed a canal system that brought water from the Colorado River and delivered it to the arid but fertile Imperial Valley of Southern

62
00:04:26,411 --> 00:04:27,111
California.

63
00:04:27,112 --> 00:04:33,500
The California development company promised bountiful harvests in what was once barren desert.

64
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:36,400
Irrigation would make it possible.

65
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:43,920
The company cut an opening in the western bank of the Colorado River and installed a headgate which controlled water flow.

66
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:49,160
Initially, the system of small gates and channels worked beyond expectation.

67
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:53,140
Fields produced a bounty of fruit and vegetables.

68
00:04:53,141 --> 00:04:58,140
It appeared as though the canal system was a major success.

69
00:04:58,141 --> 00:05:05,140
Soon thousands of people flocked to the Imperial Valley with dreams of finding a new Eden.

70
00:05:05,141 --> 00:05:07,140
But their dreams soon turned into a nightmare.

71
00:05:07,141 --> 00:05:17,140
After several years of prosperity, the unpredictable Colorado unleashed its vengeance, reminding settlers how dangerous it was to play with nature's plumbing.

72
00:05:17,141 --> 00:05:21,540
The problem was these were not very well-engineered works, and they weren't very good.

73
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:25,900
And the Colorado River in 1905 had a major flood come down the river,

74
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:31,500
and it washed out these headworks and flowed into the Imperial Valley for 18 months.

75
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:37,020
Essentially created what is today the Salton Sea, destroyed much of the agricultural land,

76
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:40,700
destroyed homes, destroyed businesses, really wreaked havoc.

77
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:44,480
Experts predicted the Salton Sea would continue to grow,

78
00:05:44,940 --> 00:05:48,400
eventually drowning millions of acres of California and Arizona.

79
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:50,420
Something had to be done.

80
00:05:51,460 --> 00:05:54,340
In 1910, the federal government dammed the breach,

81
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:56,760
but it was only a temporary solution.

82
00:05:58,220 --> 00:06:02,120
What was needed was a dam, a giant dam.

83
00:06:02,980 --> 00:06:07,240
The U.S. Bureau of Reclamation initiated a study to determine feasibility.

84
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:12,780
In 1922, Arthur Powell Davis, director of the Bureau of Reclamation,

85
00:06:12,781 --> 00:06:16,660
recommended a series of reservoirs to control the entire river.

86
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:21,920
Known as the Fall Davis Report, the centerpiece of the plan was a colossal dam

87
00:06:21,921 --> 00:06:26,020
to be built in Boulder Canyon between Nevada and Arizona.

88
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:33,200
Before the measure could proceed, the states had to decide on an equitable division of water.

89
00:06:34,300 --> 00:06:40,640
Water meant agriculture, cities, industry, growth,

90
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:44,360
And inevitably, there was some disagreement.

91
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:50,200
Arizona believed California was trying to steal an unfair share of water.

92
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:54,480
There's always a fight over water in the West.

93
00:06:54,840 --> 00:07:01,600
When water is scarce, there's going to be tension over water, and that was the case years ago.

94
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:02,720
It's still the case today.

95
00:07:03,320 --> 00:07:09,400
Herbert Hoover, then Secretary of Commerce, stepped in to break the stalemate that developed between the states.

96
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:14,760
In 1922, seven states signed the Colorado River Compact,

97
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:17,440
which divided water rights to the river.

98
00:07:18,240 --> 00:07:22,500
However, the Arizona state legislature, still not satisfied,

99
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:25,880
successfully blocked legislation for several years.

100
00:07:26,340 --> 00:07:31,900
But finally, in 1929, the Boulder Canyon Project Act passed in Congress.

101
00:07:32,140 --> 00:07:37,360
It called for a high dam near a small railroad town named Las Vegas, Nevada.

102
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:43,060
The Bureau of Reclamation worked to design a dam like no other.

103
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:49,320
The challenge was to design a structure capable of holding back trillions of gallons of water,

104
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:53,160
built in a very high yet narrow canyon.

105
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:57,700
They considered 30 different designs before finding the answer.

106
00:07:58,360 --> 00:08:01,900
The final plan, a mammoth arched gravity dam.

107
00:08:01,901 --> 00:08:10,480
Hoover Dam is a combination of gravity and arch dam.

108
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:14,060
Gravity dams are dams that are just so massive,

109
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:19,820
their weight of them themselves can hold back the weight of the water behind them.

110
00:08:20,220 --> 00:08:24,440
And then arch dams traditionally are built in the shape of an arch.

111
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:25,700
It's kind of like an eggshell.

112
00:08:25,860 --> 00:08:29,420
You know, an eggshell is very thin, but the egg itself is very strong.

113
00:08:29,421 --> 00:08:33,140
If you try to press in on the two ends of an egg, you'll find that you can't crush an egg.

114
00:08:33,240 --> 00:08:34,600
It's the strength of the arch.

115
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:42,000
The Bureau of Reclamation's planned arched gravity dam would be a behemoth,

116
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:45,700
as tall as a 70-story skyscraper,

117
00:08:46,420 --> 00:08:49,300
over two football fields thick at the base,

118
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:52,860
and capable of storing trillions of gallons of water.

119
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:56,120
It would not only be the largest dam in the world,

120
00:08:56,121 --> 00:09:01,920
it would easily be the single biggest construction project in the history of the United States.

121
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:07,100
But the immense size of the proposed dam was not the only obstacle.

122
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:11,760
The dam would be located in one of the most desolate areas in the world.

123
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:14,440
There was nothing here.

124
00:09:14,740 --> 00:09:22,140
I mean, I sometimes question the audacity of the people who had the courage and the conviction

125
00:09:22,141 --> 00:09:25,760
to think they could build a dam out in the middle of a desert where nothing existed.

126
00:09:25,761 --> 00:09:29,640
There were no roads to the site. There was no power to the site.

127
00:09:30,260 --> 00:09:31,960
There was no labor force here.

128
00:09:32,540 --> 00:09:36,040
There was nowhere for the people to live who were going to come and build the dam.

129
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:43,240
In spite of all the obstacles, the dam had to be perfect, capable of handling even the greatest floods.

130
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:48,740
If it were to fail, a wall of water would race down the river, killing thousands.

131
00:09:48,741 --> 00:09:55,740
surveyors decided to move the location from Boulder Canyon to Black Canyon, eight miles downstream.

132
00:09:55,741 --> 00:10:02,740
The move would allow for a bigger body of water, the largest man-made reservoir in the world.

133
00:10:02,741 --> 00:10:06,740
Congress gave the go-ahead to begin construction.

134
00:10:06,741 --> 00:10:11,740
The task was great. Hire an army of thousands.

135
00:10:11,741 --> 00:10:20,740
divert the river around the dam site and form a mountain of concrete into a dam in the middle of nowhere.

136
00:10:20,741 --> 00:10:24,740
The question remained, could it be done?

137
00:10:26,740 --> 00:10:33,740
Lake Mead, the reservoir created by Hoover Dam, contains enough water to cover the entire state of Pennsylvania.

138
00:10:33,741 --> 00:10:38,740
With one foot of water, Hoover Dam will return on Modern Marvels.

139
00:10:41,740 --> 00:10:47,880
In 1930, the Bureau of Reclamation's plan for controlling the Colorado River was extensive.

140
00:10:48,680 --> 00:10:52,160
The government would provide materials and certain services for the dam,

141
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:57,660
but the dam would be built almost entirely by the lowest bidder from the private sector.

142
00:10:58,320 --> 00:11:03,220
But before the lowest bidder was determined, the federal government began working on the project.

143
00:11:03,660 --> 00:11:06,880
Good news for a nation entering the Great Depression.

144
00:11:07,860 --> 00:11:13,200
The Bureau of Reclamation's first objective was to connect by rail Las Vegas to Black Canyon,

145
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:15,420
the site chosen for the dam.

146
00:11:16,460 --> 00:11:19,680
Although the site had changed from Boulder Canyon to Black Canyon,

147
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:23,200
the endeavor was still called the Boulder Canyon Project.

148
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,560
People naturally anticipated the name would be Boulder Dam.

149
00:11:28,620 --> 00:11:31,720
The location switch caused some confusion,

150
00:11:31,721 --> 00:11:34,540
but at a railroad spike driving ceremony,

151
00:11:34,541 --> 00:11:40,260
Ray Wilbur, Secretary of the Interior under President Herbert Hoover, set the record straight.

152
00:11:42,160 --> 00:11:47,200
Secretary of the Interior Ray Lyman Wilbur came out to drive the Silver Spike

153
00:11:47,201 --> 00:11:52,460
to begin construction of the railroad spur from Las Vegas out to Boulder City town site.

154
00:11:53,180 --> 00:11:56,940
And that day is the day that he announced, well, we're going to call it Hoover Dam.

155
00:11:57,460 --> 00:12:00,540
Well, this was a year after the stock market had crashed

156
00:12:00,541 --> 00:12:02,700
and the Depression was settling in

157
00:12:02,701 --> 00:12:04,640
and people were not at all happy with Hoover.

158
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:08,340
Despite the public's disapproval of President Hoover,

159
00:12:08,520 --> 00:12:10,420
they welcomed the job opportunities.

160
00:12:11,340 --> 00:12:12,900
People were desperate for work.

161
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:16,680
Soon, thousands poured into the Las Vegas area

162
00:12:16,681 --> 00:12:18,580
in hopes of finding employment.

163
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:21,260
Before the construction started,

164
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:23,040
before they hired anybody,

165
00:12:23,041 --> 00:12:26,880
they came because they knew there was a chance of a job,

166
00:12:27,240 --> 00:12:29,180
just a rumor of a job.

167
00:12:29,181 --> 00:12:36,720
Before any social programs, the only choice the unemployed had was to search out work, wherever it might be.

168
00:12:37,900 --> 00:12:43,220
Unfortunately, the dam was to be built in one of the most inhospitable climates in the world,

169
00:12:43,780 --> 00:12:49,680
where temperatures regularly reached 120 degrees and shelter simply didn't exist.

170
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:56,320
Desperate people brought their entire families, set up makeshift camps and waited for work.

171
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,020
Cardboard and canvas changed cars into campers.

172
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:04,900
Without any place to go and nowhere to stay, families lived in their cars.

173
00:13:05,620 --> 00:13:09,180
Ragtown was the fitting name given to one of the earliest camps.

174
00:13:09,980 --> 00:13:15,060
Located at the mouth of the High Canyon, Ragtown was also one of the hottest areas.

175
00:13:15,940 --> 00:13:21,480
Motivated by the ever-increasing unemployment rate, government work began as soon as funds were available.

176
00:13:21,481 --> 00:13:28,680
On January 10, 1931, the true scope of the project became public knowledge.

177
00:13:29,560 --> 00:13:33,240
The Bureau of Reclamation supplied plans to anyone interested,

178
00:13:33,820 --> 00:13:38,560
and it seemed everyone was interested in the largest government contract ever issued.

179
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:44,240
The blueprints detailed an immense structure, taller than any dam in the world,

180
00:13:45,220 --> 00:13:49,900
726 feet in height, and nearly as thick at the base.

181
00:13:49,901 --> 00:13:58,900
The process of dam building called for tremendous diversion tunnels, 50 feet in diameter, to alter the course of the river.

182
00:13:58,901 --> 00:14:07,900
Temporary coffer dams to provide a dry work area, and the pouring of three and a quarter million cubic yards of concrete.

183
00:14:07,901 --> 00:14:11,900
Nothing existed at the dam site.

184
00:14:11,901 --> 00:14:20,900
Concrete mixing plants, cooling plants, living quarters for 5,000 men and their families.

185
00:14:20,901 --> 00:14:25,900
Cableways and metalworks had to be constructed.

186
00:14:25,901 --> 00:14:31,900
The sheer size of the project intimidated even the most confident contractors.

187
00:14:31,901 --> 00:14:37,900
Build the largest dam in the world in the middle of the desert and do it in less than seven years.

188
00:14:37,901 --> 00:14:39,900
The government imposed deadline.

189
00:14:39,901 --> 00:14:44,060
There wasn't any company in the United States that was big enough to handle the dam.

190
00:14:44,560 --> 00:14:50,300
So there were six contractors that got together and formed the consortium of six companies.

191
00:14:50,301 --> 00:14:54,320
Six Companies was composed of six giant construction firms,

192
00:14:54,680 --> 00:14:58,260
united for the sole purpose of bidding on the Boulder Canyon project.

193
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:02,080
Each of the firms was familiar with big projects.

194
00:15:02,940 --> 00:15:09,400
They independently shaped the West, building railroads, tunnels, bridges and highways,

195
00:15:09,900 --> 00:15:16,420
Between the six, perhaps they had enough experience and resources to build a dam of such great magnitude.

196
00:15:16,900 --> 00:15:21,880
But they needed someone who was respected in the field of dam construction to oversee the project.

197
00:15:22,580 --> 00:15:26,060
Someone the Bureau of Reclamation would feel confident could get the job done.

198
00:15:26,500 --> 00:15:32,340
They chose Frank Crow to be their foreman here, the superintendent of the job.

199
00:15:32,660 --> 00:15:34,400
And he was a hands-on foreman.

200
00:15:35,160 --> 00:15:38,320
He didn't spend much time behind his desk.

201
00:15:38,321 --> 00:15:41,940
Frank Crowe was the most respected dam builder in America.

202
00:15:42,540 --> 00:15:46,840
With him on board, six companies was ready to make a bid for the dam construction.

203
00:15:47,680 --> 00:15:53,340
On March 4th, 1931, the Bureau of Reclamation opened sealed bids.

204
00:15:54,180 --> 00:15:59,200
Of the hundreds of interested parties, there were only a handful of qualified bidders.

205
00:16:01,080 --> 00:16:04,300
Tension ran high as each of the bids were opened.

206
00:16:04,301 --> 00:16:14,140
The six companies firm won the contract with the lowest bid of $48,890,955.

207
00:16:14,141 --> 00:16:19,300
It was the largest government contract ever awarded.

208
00:16:19,301 --> 00:16:25,300
Blasting began almost immediately.

209
00:16:25,301 --> 00:16:30,300
Six companies had their work cut out for them.

210
00:16:30,301 --> 00:16:33,300
Initially, even getting to the dam site was an obstacle.

211
00:16:33,301 --> 00:16:36,300
There were no roads from the rim to the bottom of the canyon.

212
00:16:36,301 --> 00:16:39,300
Men instead took small ferries to the dam site.

213
00:16:39,301 --> 00:16:45,300
Later, cableways were erected across the canyon to get men and materials to the bottom.

214
00:16:45,301 --> 00:16:52,300
The first monumental task was diverting the river around the dam site.

215
00:16:52,301 --> 00:16:56,300
Engineers needed a dry work area in which to set the dam.

216
00:16:56,301 --> 00:17:02,300
To add to the pressure, the Bureau of Reclamation initiated stiff fines for missed deadlines

217
00:17:02,301 --> 00:17:05,042
deadlines at each stage of construction.

218
00:17:05,043 --> 00:17:08,400
Four tunnels had to be drilled through the canyon

219
00:17:08,401 --> 00:17:10,478
walls to circumvent the site.

220
00:17:10,479 --> 00:17:14,420
The gigantic 56-foot tunnels averaged three-quarters of

221
00:17:14,421 --> 00:17:19,812
a mile in length and would carry the entire volume of the Colorado River.

222
00:17:19,813 --> 00:17:20,920
Workmen drilled

223
00:17:20,921 --> 00:17:24,014
blasting holes and loaded them with dynamite.

224
00:17:24,015 --> 00:17:27,040
A special three-story truck holding drillers

225
00:17:27,041 --> 00:17:29,680
at three different levels expedited the process.

226
00:17:30,880 --> 00:17:36,480
Digging continued for 13 months, drilling, blasting,

227
00:17:37,360 --> 00:17:40,920
and removing millions of cubic yards of debris.

228
00:17:41,720 --> 00:17:44,680
An endless stream of trucks removed blasted rock

229
00:17:44,681 --> 00:17:48,820
from the diversion tunnels and labored up a steep incline for disposal.

230
00:17:49,480 --> 00:17:52,640
Three shifts of men were 24 hours a day,

231
00:17:53,260 --> 00:17:56,500
seven days a week, to keep the tunnels on schedule.

232
00:17:57,040 --> 00:18:00,020
Frank Crow was responsible for keeping things moving.

233
00:18:00,940 --> 00:18:04,160
The hectic pace of construction had its consequences.

234
00:18:04,860 --> 00:18:07,740
Heat exhaustion cost 14 men their lives.

235
00:18:08,740 --> 00:18:12,560
After the Depression and the crash, it was so difficult to get a job.

236
00:18:13,040 --> 00:18:17,260
Companies like the six companies could easily afford to cut a lot of corners

237
00:18:17,261 --> 00:18:20,120
in terms of safety and health of their workers,

238
00:18:20,121 --> 00:18:23,980
in terms of pay, in terms of comfort and that kind of thing, and they did.

239
00:18:24,680 --> 00:18:28,380
Tensions came to a head in the scorching summer of 1931.

240
00:18:29,040 --> 00:18:34,260
Striking workers demanded better working conditions, fresh water and improved safety precautions.

241
00:18:35,220 --> 00:18:42,380
Six companies responded by firing 1,400 strikers and hiring fresh workers readily supplied by the Depression.

242
00:18:43,040 --> 00:18:48,800
Frank Crowe was known as a fair man, but his hands were tied by the other members of six companies.

243
00:18:49,640 --> 00:18:53,420
However, soon after the strike, working conditions began to improve.

244
00:18:53,980 --> 00:19:01,120
In the end, when confronted with having a rough job or going hungry, most opted for the employment.

245
00:19:02,600 --> 00:19:10,140
I came here in August in 1931, and I worked as a truck driver down at the dam site.

246
00:19:10,141 --> 00:19:16,420
I remember one stretch of 10 months without a day off, and we were working seven days a week.

247
00:19:17,540 --> 00:19:21,940
And the pay was, to me, was great. It was $5 a day.

248
00:19:22,500 --> 00:19:26,240
More than 5,000 men worked on the dam at the height of construction.

249
00:19:27,020 --> 00:19:30,720
Frank Crow, anxious about deadlines, often questioned their output.

250
00:19:31,660 --> 00:19:35,240
He used to take the VIPs around and give them a tour.

251
00:19:35,241 --> 00:19:41,000
Well, one of these wives of one of the people that he was giving the grand tour said,

252
00:19:41,400 --> 00:19:44,940
Mr. Crow, how many men do you have working here?

253
00:19:45,380 --> 00:19:47,640
He said, about half of them.

254
00:19:47,641 --> 00:19:52,640
But Frank Crowe didn't kid when it came to headlines.

255
00:19:52,641 --> 00:19:58,640
His hard-working team completed the diversion tunnels months ahead of schedule, in only 14 months.

256
00:19:58,641 --> 00:20:03,640
After digging the diversion tunnels, workers lined them with three feet of concrete.

257
00:20:03,641 --> 00:20:08,640
Special forms of gigantic proportion moved along the interior of the tunnels.

258
00:20:08,641 --> 00:20:11,640
But the job had just begun.

259
00:20:11,641 --> 00:20:14,640
They still had to force the river through the tunnels,

260
00:20:14,641 --> 00:20:19,840
build the powerhouse, intake tunnels, and the colossal dam itself.

261
00:20:19,841 --> 00:20:23,140
The most dangerous stage of construction was completed,

262
00:20:23,141 --> 00:20:26,640
but the most spectacular work was yet to come.

263
00:20:26,641 --> 00:20:31,640
112 people lost their lives working on Hoover Dam.

264
00:20:31,641 --> 00:20:36,547
Ironically, the first and last persons killed, J.G. Tierney and P.W.

265
00:20:36,548 --> 00:20:38,640
Tierney, were father and son.

266
00:20:38,641 --> 00:20:42,640
Hoover Dam will continue on Modern Marvels.

267
00:20:44,640 --> 00:20:48,240
It wasn't machines that built Hoover Dam, it was men.

268
00:20:48,880 --> 00:20:50,680
People with different job titles.

269
00:20:51,180 --> 00:20:54,640
Powder monkeys, muckers and high scalers.

270
00:20:54,641 --> 00:20:56,800
A labor force of thousands.

271
00:20:57,580 --> 00:20:58,280
Blasted.

272
00:20:59,120 --> 00:20:59,820
Dug.

273
00:21:00,920 --> 00:21:01,620
Pauld.

274
00:21:02,460 --> 00:21:03,160
Poured.

275
00:21:03,820 --> 00:21:04,520
Dumped.

276
00:21:05,140 --> 00:21:05,840
Loaded.

277
00:21:06,180 --> 00:21:09,020
And cleared the site in preparation for the dam.

278
00:21:10,020 --> 00:21:13,460
As work on the diversion tunnels progressed down below,

279
00:21:13,461 --> 00:21:16,700
more breathtaking work took place high above.

280
00:21:17,980 --> 00:21:22,020
Canyon walls required smoothing and shaping for the abutting dam.

281
00:21:22,580 --> 00:21:27,520
Men blasted thousands of cubic yards of rock from the sheer canyon faces.

282
00:21:28,300 --> 00:21:34,240
In daring acrobatic displays, high scalers swung from ropes to accomplish the bold feat.

283
00:21:35,080 --> 00:21:39,740
That was a job that I imagined and just freak about it.

284
00:21:39,741 --> 00:21:43,240
And we're talking about a canyon that's 700 and some feet deep.

285
00:21:43,460 --> 00:21:45,820
from the top to solid rock at the bottom.

286
00:21:47,260 --> 00:21:49,880
They would hang on what they called bosun's chairs,

287
00:21:50,060 --> 00:21:51,260
just little wooden chairs.

288
00:21:51,940 --> 00:21:54,820
They would drill the holes that would later be blasted.

289
00:21:55,060 --> 00:21:56,800
Then they would go back down with their pikes

290
00:21:56,801 --> 00:21:58,720
and scale off all of that rock.

291
00:21:59,440 --> 00:22:00,620
And that was their job.

292
00:22:01,320 --> 00:22:02,840
When dam construction began,

293
00:22:02,841 --> 00:22:06,160
the government wanted the high scalers and all employees

294
00:22:06,161 --> 00:22:08,060
out of the squatter-style camps

295
00:22:08,061 --> 00:22:10,000
and into more suitable accommodations.

296
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:15,780
Six companies labored to provide housing for their workers as quickly as possible

297
00:22:15,781 --> 00:22:19,780
and built Boulder City, seven miles from the work site.

298
00:22:21,340 --> 00:22:24,320
Men framed as many as three houses a day,

299
00:22:24,760 --> 00:22:29,280
and stories abounded of people coming home from work and mistakenly entering the wrong home.

300
00:22:30,460 --> 00:22:33,840
They built everything that a city should have.

301
00:22:34,220 --> 00:22:37,320
It would have a movie theater, it would have entertainment,

302
00:22:37,321 --> 00:22:39,100
It would have schools for the children.

303
00:22:39,600 --> 00:22:41,920
It would have churches for people to go to.

304
00:22:42,640 --> 00:22:45,840
Boulder City was a normal city in all senses,

305
00:22:46,240 --> 00:22:48,420
except that it was a government reservation.

306
00:22:49,200 --> 00:22:51,780
The federal government had full authority in the town.

307
00:22:52,960 --> 00:22:55,200
The Secretary of the Interior, Ray Wilbur,

308
00:22:55,320 --> 00:22:59,160
appointed Sims Ely to manage the city of 7,000 residents.

309
00:22:59,600 --> 00:23:01,680
A man who commanded respect.

310
00:23:01,880 --> 00:23:07,140
Sims Ely acted as judge, mayor, and chamber of commerce.

311
00:23:07,320 --> 00:23:13,120
He decided who would be given business licenses and what type of business they could have.

312
00:23:13,560 --> 00:23:18,920
Unlike many others during the Depression, people in Boulder City had a little money to spend.

313
00:23:19,600 --> 00:23:22,120
By choosing which businesses were allowed in town,

314
00:23:22,300 --> 00:23:26,480
Sim Zeely determined for his workers how to spend their money more responsibly.

315
00:23:27,560 --> 00:23:32,280
Six companies paid their workers half in real money and half in script.

316
00:23:32,760 --> 00:23:37,020
The only place you could spend this script was in a six-company store.

317
00:23:37,320 --> 00:23:42,700
As city manager, Sims Ely was also responsible for enforcing the law.

318
00:23:43,560 --> 00:23:47,520
There would be no alcohol or gambling within the city limits.

319
00:23:48,340 --> 00:23:53,400
Responsible for several thousand young men, Ely had a big chore on his hands.

320
00:23:54,020 --> 00:23:58,040
But federal marshals helped him with the seemingly impossible task.

321
00:23:58,560 --> 00:24:00,740
He was wonderful. He was mean.

322
00:24:00,741 --> 00:24:03,660
He just didn't do anything wrong. He found out.

323
00:24:03,980 --> 00:24:07,140
It immediately swundered me. I wasn't pulled up in front of him.

324
00:24:07,320 --> 00:24:11,780
With a seven-day-a-week work schedule, there wasn't much time for recreation.

325
00:24:12,720 --> 00:24:16,720
But the single, the energetic, and the organized made time.

326
00:24:18,020 --> 00:24:24,540
Alcohol and gambling weren't permitted in Boulder City, but just about everything was in Las Vegas.

327
00:24:25,600 --> 00:24:31,660
On payday, many men worked their shift, drove into Las Vegas, stayed out all night,

328
00:24:32,020 --> 00:24:34,640
then returned to their shift the next morning.

329
00:24:35,260 --> 00:24:40,780
If you wanted real fun, true fun, you went down the hill into Las Vegas,

330
00:24:41,020 --> 00:24:45,040
where you had gambling and you had prostitution and you had all the liquor you could drink.

331
00:24:46,180 --> 00:24:51,420
Only 30 miles away, Las Vegas offered an escape from the rigid rules of Boulder City.

332
00:24:52,200 --> 00:24:54,880
Boulder fueled the commerce and growth of Las Vegas.

333
00:24:55,720 --> 00:25:01,540
And like the international attraction it is today, Las Vegas accepted all varieties of currency.

334
00:25:01,960 --> 00:25:06,180
The workers would go down to Las Vegas onto Block 16, which is the red light district,

335
00:25:06,520 --> 00:25:08,240
and they would drink and they would gamble,

336
00:25:08,700 --> 00:25:12,960
and they would get serviced in the houses of prostitution along Block 16,

337
00:25:13,160 --> 00:25:14,860
and they would pay for it with the script.

338
00:25:15,340 --> 00:25:20,060
Then the prostitutes would take the script there and come back up to Boulder City with it and go shopping.

339
00:25:20,660 --> 00:25:25,980
Being 30 miles from Las Vegas emphasized the fact that Boulder City was a haven.

340
00:25:26,940 --> 00:25:30,580
There was no crime in Boulder City, and to many it was a paradise,

341
00:25:30,581 --> 00:25:34,620
a sanctuary free from the evils of the outside world.

342
00:25:35,200 --> 00:25:36,180
It was a model town.

343
00:25:36,460 --> 00:25:37,520
It was perfect.

344
00:25:38,080 --> 00:25:39,540
It was meant to be perfect.

345
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:43,260
You couldn't have any kind of alcohol.

346
00:25:43,440 --> 00:25:44,140
You better not.

347
00:25:44,500 --> 00:25:45,940
They had a gate outside town,

348
00:25:46,300 --> 00:25:48,420
and if you got stuck there and had alcohol,

349
00:25:48,600 --> 00:25:50,320
they rested you on the spot.

350
00:25:51,460 --> 00:25:54,720
We didn't know anybody in poverty.

351
00:25:55,100 --> 00:25:57,600
If someone wasn't working, they weren't here.

352
00:25:57,601 --> 00:26:01,380
and so we didn't know what poverty was.

353
00:26:02,580 --> 00:26:06,080
Boulder City offered security, shelter, and a full belly.

354
00:26:06,680 --> 00:26:08,420
Stories about food were legendary.

355
00:26:09,320 --> 00:26:11,480
Food was good and there was plenty of it.

356
00:26:12,080 --> 00:26:13,200
When others were starving,

357
00:26:13,520 --> 00:26:17,120
workers in Boulder City feasted on three square meals a day.

358
00:26:17,780 --> 00:26:19,280
The Anderson Brothers mess hall

359
00:26:19,281 --> 00:26:21,760
served as many as 1,300 men proceeding.

360
00:26:22,660 --> 00:26:24,780
The staff of kitchen help scurried around

361
00:26:24,781 --> 00:26:26,180
in a flurry of activity,

362
00:26:26,181 --> 00:26:29,300
preparing as many as 6,000 meals a day.

363
00:26:30,740 --> 00:26:33,340
Boulder City was more than a construction camp.

364
00:26:33,800 --> 00:26:36,240
It was a place where people could get a new start.

365
00:26:37,060 --> 00:26:39,520
When they came to Boulder City that was brand new,

366
00:26:39,521 --> 00:26:45,220
then they had an opportunity to build a whole civic structure from scratch.

367
00:26:45,600 --> 00:26:48,060
But Boulder City wasn't for everyone.

368
00:26:48,660 --> 00:26:53,420
Typical of 1930s America, segregation was the rule.

369
00:26:53,800 --> 00:26:55,560
It was supposed to be a perfect town,

370
00:26:55,561 --> 00:27:01,731
The segregation was tremendous, too. They had one black crew that worked on the dam.

371
00:27:01,732 --> 00:27:07,240
There was about 14 or 15 members. They could not even live in Boulder City.

372
00:27:07,660 --> 00:27:12,078
Six companies did the hiring. Less than 1% of dam employees were black.

373
00:27:12,079 --> 00:27:16,620
The few who did work on the dam were given the least desirable positions.

374
00:27:16,621 --> 00:27:22,540
The new Secretary of the Interior, Harold Ickes, hoped to alter the situation

375
00:27:22,541 --> 00:27:26,860
But he didn't have authority to change six companies' hiring practices

376
00:27:26,861 --> 00:27:30,660
He did, however, have authority in Boulder City

377
00:27:30,661 --> 00:27:36,240
He announced in 1933 that the few black employees could live there

378
00:27:36,241 --> 00:27:42,680
It was a minor victory for the black employees who found discrimination a way of life

379
00:27:42,681 --> 00:27:47,660
Herbert Hoover, champion and namesake of the dam

380
00:27:47,661 --> 00:27:49,500
Only visited it once

381
00:27:49,501 --> 00:27:51,200
In 1932

382
00:27:51,201 --> 00:27:53,140
Hoover Dam will return

383
00:27:53,141 --> 00:27:55,660
On Modern Marvels

384
00:27:55,661 --> 00:28:00,360
While Herbert Hoover's administration began Hoover Dam

385
00:28:00,361 --> 00:28:03,340
Franklin Delano Roosevelt's had to finish it

386
00:28:03,341 --> 00:28:06,960
Hoover lost in a landslide to Roosevelt in his new deal

387
00:28:06,961 --> 00:28:10,100
But the new president continued supporting the dam

388
00:28:10,101 --> 00:28:11,840
And the thousands it employed

389
00:28:12,580 --> 00:28:16,400
What Roosevelt didn't like was the name, Hoover Dam.

390
00:28:17,200 --> 00:28:21,920
Named after the former president, Hoover Dam had a serious identity crisis.

391
00:28:22,440 --> 00:28:27,360
Originally called Boulder Dam, even though it wasn't built in Boulder Canyon, but in Black Canyon.

392
00:28:27,361 --> 00:28:36,860
Then changed to Hoover Dam, Harold Ickes announced on May 8, 1933, the name would change yet again back to Boulder Dam.

393
00:28:36,861 --> 00:28:42,840
Fortunately, the great name debate didn't impede progress on the dam

394
00:28:42,841 --> 00:28:50,400
On November 14, 1932, water began flowing through the great diversion tunnels

395
00:28:50,401 --> 00:28:56,920
Men successfully forced the entire Colorado River from its ancient bed

396
00:28:56,921 --> 00:29:02,660
Two temporary dams called coffer dams were erected to create a dry work area

397
00:29:02,661 --> 00:29:07,880
one just below the diversion tunnel's inlets and the other just above the outlets

398
00:29:07,881 --> 00:29:10,420
so water wouldn't back up into the site.

399
00:29:11,420 --> 00:29:14,500
For five months, trucks dumped rock into the river,

400
00:29:15,120 --> 00:29:17,140
forming two enormous dams,

401
00:29:17,580 --> 00:29:22,880
the larger one being 100 feet tall and 750 feet thick at the base.

402
00:29:23,720 --> 00:29:28,040
Water was then pumped from between the dams and excavation began.

403
00:29:28,041 --> 00:29:33,040
Excavation 2 was a monumental undertaking.

404
00:29:33,041 --> 00:29:39,040
More than 40 feet of rock, sand and silt had to be removed from the now dry riverbed.

405
00:29:39,041 --> 00:29:46,040
Steam shovels dredged a half million cubic yards of material in preparation for pouring concrete.

406
00:29:46,041 --> 00:29:55,040
And on June 6, 1933, the first bucket of concrete poured into the form at the bottom of the canyon.

407
00:29:55,041 --> 00:29:59,240
Producing enough concrete for the dam was a problem in itself.

408
00:29:59,241 --> 00:30:06,040
Hoover Dam required nearly as much concrete as all previous Bureau of Reclamation dams combined.

409
00:30:06,041 --> 00:30:14,040
A two-lane highway from Los Angeles to Boston could be built with the concrete used on Hoover Dam.

410
00:30:14,041 --> 00:30:17,040
A nearby riverbed provided the gravel.

411
00:30:17,041 --> 00:30:25,120
Men loaded material into train cars and transported it to the separating plant, where it was divided by size.

412
00:30:26,040 --> 00:30:32,200
Trains then hauled gravel to the two mixing facilities built to produce the tremendous amount of concrete.

413
00:30:33,600 --> 00:30:43,760
Night and day, concrete left the mixing plant, truckload after truckload, bucket after bucket, for placement in the dam forms.

414
00:30:43,761 --> 00:30:47,360
it's not one monolithic piece

415
00:30:47,361 --> 00:30:49,440
it's poured more like a kid's Lego toy

416
00:30:49,441 --> 00:30:51,280
is locked together like that

417
00:30:51,281 --> 00:30:53,460
and they poured grout in between those

418
00:30:53,461 --> 00:30:55,920
so they poured it in individual blocks

419
00:30:55,921 --> 00:30:58,360
the dam was poured in forms

420
00:30:58,361 --> 00:31:00,500
from 25 to 60 feet square

421
00:31:00,501 --> 00:31:01,600
and 5 feet deep

422
00:31:01,601 --> 00:31:04,140
if poured as one massive block

423
00:31:04,141 --> 00:31:06,640
not only would it be inclined to crack

424
00:31:06,641 --> 00:31:10,200
but it would take over 125 years

425
00:31:10,201 --> 00:31:11,760
to completely harden

426
00:31:11,761 --> 00:31:16,960
The chemical reaction of hardening concrete also produced heat.

427
00:31:16,961 --> 00:31:20,860
Too much heat would cause the huge concrete blocks to crack.

428
00:31:20,861 --> 00:31:27,260
So steel pipes were inserted into the wet concrete and cold water pumped through the pipes

429
00:31:27,261 --> 00:31:31,260
to slow the curing process and reduce cracking.

430
00:31:31,261 --> 00:31:37,260
After a few days of hardening, a block could be poured on top or adjacent to it.

431
00:31:37,261 --> 00:31:43,600
A project of such magnitude and strict deadlines required innovative construction techniques.

432
00:31:44,360 --> 00:31:48,260
Frank Crow pioneered the extensive use of cableways on earlier projects,

433
00:31:49,180 --> 00:31:52,100
but never before had cableways been used so efficiently.

434
00:31:52,660 --> 00:31:58,560
From one-eighth of a mile above, cableway operators choreographed a ballet between men and machine.

435
00:31:59,560 --> 00:32:03,220
Train cars transported concrete buckets from the mixing plant.

436
00:32:04,020 --> 00:32:10,560
Cableway operators lifted the 22-ton buckets and sent them gliding to the appropriate form.

437
00:32:16,620 --> 00:32:22,700
There, men released the safety locks and 16 tons of concrete poured into the form.

438
00:32:23,720 --> 00:32:31,500
Activity progressed 24 hours a day, seven days a week, pouring up to 1,100 buckets of concrete per day.

439
00:32:31,501 --> 00:32:36,600
The aerial cableways weren't the only innovations at the dam site

440
00:32:36,601 --> 00:32:40,560
We believe that this is where the hard hat was invented

441
00:32:40,561 --> 00:32:42,620
That everybody uses nowadays

442
00:32:42,621 --> 00:32:47,420
The construction workers have been faced with debris falling on them from above

443
00:32:47,421 --> 00:32:51,500
And they realized that they were going to have to do something

444
00:32:51,501 --> 00:32:53,680
And so what they did was

445
00:32:53,681 --> 00:32:57,460
They put two baseball caps together like this

446
00:32:57,461 --> 00:33:00,140
With the bill going this way and this way

447
00:33:00,141 --> 00:33:04,120
and then they took and dipped it in tar

448
00:33:04,121 --> 00:33:06,660
and took it out and dipped it in tar

449
00:33:06,661 --> 00:33:08,760
and let it harden and dipped it in tar

450
00:33:08,761 --> 00:33:09,620
and let it harden.

451
00:33:09,940 --> 00:33:11,500
And these were all homemade hats.

452
00:33:11,680 --> 00:33:13,720
The company didn't have anything to do with this.

453
00:33:14,680 --> 00:33:17,020
But when several men wearing the homemade hats

454
00:33:17,021 --> 00:33:18,680
were saved from falling debris,

455
00:33:19,280 --> 00:33:22,400
six companies purchased thousands of hard-boiled hats

456
00:33:22,401 --> 00:33:23,300
for their employees.

457
00:33:24,180 --> 00:33:26,040
As the dam steadily grew in height,

458
00:33:26,041 --> 00:33:29,460
the splendor of its design was undeniable.

459
00:33:29,461 --> 00:33:38,440
When you think of the construction of that dam, most of us, we were working specifically at some particular job,

460
00:33:38,520 --> 00:33:44,280
and we really didn't have a picture of what was happening around here or what was going to happen.

461
00:33:45,380 --> 00:33:56,220
And to me, it was a fascinating time to begin with, to come down there every day and just see that dam gradually going up.

462
00:33:57,140 --> 00:34:00,000
Concrete pouring pushed the dam higher and higher.

463
00:34:00,680 --> 00:34:04,580
As it grew taller, so did the tails surrounding the project.

464
00:34:05,300 --> 00:34:08,160
Despite rumors, no one is buried in the concrete.

465
00:34:08,880 --> 00:34:11,880
A decomposing body would threaten the integrity of the dam.

466
00:34:11,881 --> 00:34:16,540
Besides, an average pour only increased the depth in the form a few inches.

467
00:34:17,400 --> 00:34:23,380
While the concrete was being poured, there were several ancillary projects happening simultaneously.

468
00:34:23,381 --> 00:34:27,580
Four water intake towers were built in front of the dam.

469
00:34:28,580 --> 00:34:33,060
Giant steel pipes called penstocks connected to the intake towers

470
00:34:33,061 --> 00:34:36,320
and would funnel water to the generators and through the dam.

471
00:34:36,880 --> 00:34:41,500
Penstocks provided the internal plumbing of the dam and were as large as 30 feet in diameter.

472
00:34:42,040 --> 00:34:45,840
Producing these massive pipes tested the resources of the day.

473
00:34:46,320 --> 00:34:47,680
They were too large to ship.

474
00:34:48,400 --> 00:34:52,840
Workers instead erected an entire fabrication facility at the dam.

475
00:34:53,380 --> 00:35:01,380
Giant presses rolled two and three quarter inch steel plates into 11 foot pipe sections, weighing 80 tons each.

476
00:35:01,880 --> 00:35:06,500
Two pieces combined to make one colossal section 22 feet long.

477
00:35:07,560 --> 00:35:14,600
Tractors moved the enormous sections to the canyon rim, where the largest cableway floated them to the base of the dam.

478
00:35:15,500 --> 00:35:20,780
Each section was joined to other sections forming over one mile of welded pipe.

479
00:35:20,781 --> 00:35:26,760
In front of the dam, engineers designed spillways to protect it in times of great flooding.

480
00:35:27,340 --> 00:35:30,900
Water would flow into the spillways instead of overflowing the dam.

481
00:35:31,740 --> 00:35:39,560
Only twice were the spillways used, once for testing and the other in 1983 during an exceptionally wet spring.

482
00:35:40,240 --> 00:35:45,540
That year, water flowed over the spillways at 52,000 cubic feet per second.

483
00:35:45,541 --> 00:35:52,540
Despite the record water flow, the spillways performed as designed more than a half century earlier.

484
00:35:52,541 --> 00:35:57,540
And flooding downriver was minimized by the efficiency of the Colorado River system.

485
00:35:57,541 --> 00:36:05,540
With the spillways constructed, the dam was one step closer to completion and the fulfillment of its purpose.

486
00:36:05,541 --> 00:36:10,540
But there was a lot at risk. Filling the lake would be the only way to test the dam.

487
00:36:10,541 --> 00:36:15,120
At that point, structural failure would be catastrophic.

488
00:36:16,960 --> 00:36:21,800
The weight of water filling Lake Mead caused many earthquakes in the Boulder City area,

489
00:36:22,180 --> 00:36:24,940
some measuring as great as 5.0 on the Richter scale.

490
00:36:25,340 --> 00:36:26,820
Modern marvels will return.

491
00:36:28,480 --> 00:36:30,420
The dam was nearing completion.

492
00:36:31,240 --> 00:36:32,780
Workers finished the spillways.

493
00:36:33,360 --> 00:36:35,840
Its height reached 726 feet,

494
00:36:35,841 --> 00:36:41,240
and it became obvious that the dam was more than just a six million ton chunk of concrete.

495
00:36:41,880 --> 00:36:43,300
There was an elegance to it.

496
00:36:43,920 --> 00:36:49,820
The visual with this dam, I think, is more spectacular than some of the others.

497
00:36:51,120 --> 00:36:56,940
Grand Coulee is a wonderful dam, but it's not nearly as spectacular to look at, and neither is Shasta.

498
00:36:57,480 --> 00:37:00,220
The elegance of the design was no accident.

499
00:37:01,000 --> 00:37:04,200
Notable artists brought a touch of class to Black Canyon.

500
00:37:04,201 --> 00:37:12,800
Gordon Kaufman was a very famous architect who is the one really responsible for the whole Art Deco appearance of the dam.

501
00:37:12,801 --> 00:37:20,760
He's the one that was brought in and designed the very sleek, angular geometric lines and the planes of it,

502
00:37:20,840 --> 00:37:24,220
which make it such an interesting and unusual structure in itself.

503
00:37:25,280 --> 00:37:31,980
The Art Deco design is found on the inside as well, from the elegant visitor elevators

504
00:37:31,981 --> 00:37:34,680
and aluminum doors,

505
00:37:36,280 --> 00:37:38,440
decorative railings in the powerhouse.

506
00:37:40,540 --> 00:37:43,900
Terrazzo floors inspired by Native American design

507
00:37:43,901 --> 00:37:45,920
symbolize the power of the dam.

508
00:37:47,440 --> 00:37:50,760
And protecting the dam are the winged figures of the Republic,

509
00:37:51,300 --> 00:37:53,540
designed by Oscar J.W. Hansen.

510
00:37:54,560 --> 00:37:56,240
By the summer of 1935,

511
00:37:56,241 --> 00:37:59,640
the men of Boulder Canyon accomplished something amazing,

512
00:38:00,620 --> 00:38:04,100
Completion of concrete pouring two years ahead of schedule.

513
00:38:04,880 --> 00:38:10,600
What stood in the narrows of Black Canyon was a monument to Frank Crow and the men who built it.

514
00:38:10,820 --> 00:38:15,800
To accomplish what Frank Crow did two years ahead of schedule and under budget,

515
00:38:16,600 --> 00:38:20,720
had to be a tremendous individual, maybe not loved by everybody,

516
00:38:21,260 --> 00:38:26,500
but certainly he set out to build the dam and he accomplished that.

517
00:38:26,501 --> 00:38:32,880
Frank Crow went on to do other projects for the government, but Hoover Dam was his crowning achievement.

518
00:38:33,080 --> 00:38:39,580
The time we completed it, I just felt that I had been a part of something that was just tremendous.

519
00:38:40,600 --> 00:38:50,680
On September 30, 1935, President Franklin Delano Roosevelt came to the desert of the Southwest to dedicate the dam that so many had worked so hard to complete.

520
00:38:50,681 --> 00:38:58,900
On that day, President Roosevelt dedicated Boulder Dam, but from its inception there was confusion over the name of the dam.

521
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:11,080
First Boulder, then Hoover, then back to Boulder, but finally, in 1947, an act of Congress changed the name back to Hoover Dam, and it remains that today.

522
00:39:12,600 --> 00:39:17,180
Four months before Roosevelt's dedication, Lake Mead began filling.

523
00:39:17,181 --> 00:39:25,340
It took six years to reach maximum capacity, and the mighty structure successfully held back trillions of gallons of water.

524
00:39:26,760 --> 00:39:29,900
Water that could now be used to produce electricity.

525
00:39:31,380 --> 00:39:38,340
Water from Lake Mead flowed in through the intake towers, to turbines which in turn rotated generators,

526
00:39:39,140 --> 00:39:46,000
producing such a massive amount of electricity that by 1987 the power produced completely paid for the dam,

527
00:39:46,001 --> 00:39:47,400
plus interest.

528
00:39:47,900 --> 00:39:49,580
We have a service area,

529
00:39:49,900 --> 00:39:52,780
including all of Southern California and Arizona,

530
00:39:53,080 --> 00:39:55,520
probably more than 20 million people

531
00:39:55,521 --> 00:39:57,320
that receive some of the power.

532
00:39:58,180 --> 00:39:59,740
The river had been tamed,

533
00:40:00,300 --> 00:40:02,220
flood devastation minimized,

534
00:40:03,000 --> 00:40:05,380
crops grew in a once barren desert,

535
00:40:05,920 --> 00:40:08,220
and free electricity was generated.

536
00:40:09,420 --> 00:40:11,680
Men had altered Mother Nature's design,

537
00:40:12,340 --> 00:40:13,740
but did they improve it?

538
00:40:13,741 --> 00:40:17,880
There are no negative effects that were known at the time that construction occurred.

539
00:40:18,040 --> 00:40:25,480
Obviously, as we've learned more about river systems and we've become more sensitive to environmental issues,

540
00:40:26,460 --> 00:40:32,260
we've learned that the natural environment is certainly affected by dams.

541
00:40:32,560 --> 00:40:37,940
Dams change the natural flow of rivers and inevitably impact plants and animals.

542
00:40:37,941 --> 00:40:40,340
More fish live here than ever could before.

543
00:40:40,720 --> 00:40:44,720
More animals, mountain sheep, birds, other animals, mountain lions,

544
00:40:44,980 --> 00:40:49,240
because the one scarce element, the one thing that was missing from this environment, water,

545
00:40:49,680 --> 00:40:52,060
is now here 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

546
00:40:52,340 --> 00:40:56,700
In spite of the abundance of wildlife, certain native species of plants and animals

547
00:40:56,701 --> 00:40:59,200
were negatively affected by damming the Colorado.

548
00:41:00,040 --> 00:41:04,980
Altering the natural habitat has led to the absence of many species within the Colorado River.

549
00:41:05,900 --> 00:41:10,960
Some native fish have found reproduction more difficult in the colder waters of Lake Mead.

550
00:41:11,580 --> 00:41:17,140
The environmental impact wasn't the primary concern for workers nearing the end of dam construction.

551
00:41:17,760 --> 00:41:20,500
Most were preoccupied with finding another job.

552
00:41:21,300 --> 00:41:24,900
When construction ended, most men left to work on other dams,

553
00:41:24,901 --> 00:41:28,680
but many stayed to work on the powerhouse and finishing touches.

554
00:41:29,760 --> 00:41:33,660
Unlike other construction camps, Boulder City didn't dry up.

555
00:41:33,661 --> 00:41:38,860
It remained a reservation until 1959 when it incorporated.

556
00:41:39,500 --> 00:41:43,760
Although the town could now permit alcohol and gaming, it didn't.

557
00:41:44,660 --> 00:41:48,140
While alcohol was finally offered for sale in 1969,

558
00:41:48,880 --> 00:41:53,540
Boulder City remains the only city in Nevada that doesn't allow gambling.

559
00:41:54,700 --> 00:42:00,660
Seven miles from Boulder City at the top of the dam sits a $125 million visitor center

560
00:42:00,661 --> 00:42:04,880
built for the more than one million visitors who stop at Hoover Dam a year.

561
00:42:05,580 --> 00:42:10,760
Constructed in 1995, architects tried to match the elegance of the dam.

562
00:42:11,780 --> 00:42:16,260
Money generated from site tours will pay for half of the visitor center's cost.

563
00:42:17,200 --> 00:42:23,160
For a few dollars, visitors get an intimate look behind the curtain of concrete.

564
00:42:23,161 --> 00:42:31,500
In addition to dam visitors, 8 million people a year enjoy the recreational benefits of Lake Mead.

565
00:42:32,160 --> 00:42:36,220
Named after Elwood Mead, the former commissioner for the Bureau of Reclamation,

566
00:42:36,820 --> 00:42:39,980
Lake Mead is the largest man-made reservoir in the country.

567
00:42:41,140 --> 00:42:44,820
A problem that accompanies many reservoir systems is silting,

568
00:42:45,300 --> 00:42:47,860
or the building up of sediment at the bottom of a reservoir.

569
00:42:48,420 --> 00:42:51,040
Flowing water carries sediment with it,

570
00:42:51,041 --> 00:42:55,400
if enough sediment collects it can fill a reservoir, rendering it useless.

571
00:42:56,300 --> 00:43:02,360
But with the help of large reservoirs like Lake Mead and 53 Bureau of Reclamation dams

572
00:43:02,361 --> 00:43:06,920
helping to trap sediment, silting is minimized on the Colorado River system.

573
00:43:07,840 --> 00:43:11,500
But the U.S. isn't the only benefactor of the Colorado's bounty.

574
00:43:12,380 --> 00:43:16,200
Despite past disagreements about water quality and quantity,

575
00:43:16,201 --> 00:43:20,680
Mexico receives 1.5 million acre-feet of water a year.

576
00:43:21,040 --> 00:43:22,620
downstream from Hoover Dam.

577
00:43:24,080 --> 00:43:27,800
Engineers designed Hoover Dam to provide many benefits,

578
00:43:28,100 --> 00:43:30,280
but they could not have foreseen the impact

579
00:43:30,281 --> 00:43:33,800
the completed project would have on people and the environment.

580
00:43:34,700 --> 00:43:36,680
From the close-knit community of Boulder City

581
00:43:36,681 --> 00:43:39,000
to California's fertile Imperial Valley,

582
00:43:39,380 --> 00:43:41,920
the Hoover Dam has changed the course of a river

583
00:43:41,921 --> 00:43:43,720
and the course of people's lives.

584
00:43:43,721 --> 00:43:48,640
I think it symbolizes what the American people were about

585
00:43:48,641 --> 00:43:51,080
in that particular time in our history.

586
00:43:51,520 --> 00:43:53,800
We were moving to new frontiers.

587
00:43:53,980 --> 00:43:55,320
We were settling the West.

588
00:43:55,460 --> 00:43:57,480
We were building a nation.

589
00:43:57,700 --> 00:43:59,000
We were building a life.

590
00:43:59,580 --> 00:44:02,080
And the attitude was, we can do this.

591
00:44:02,700 --> 00:44:03,840
And they did.

